    "body": "Image created using AI\nBy Charlotte Goldstone\n19 November 2026\nUS shippers using road haulage are increasingly abandoning annual freight contracts in favour of shorter-term agreements, as soaring diesel prices outpace freight rate increases, according to new research from EASE Logistics.\nThe Ohio-based 3PL and 4PL provider’s Q3 26 Automotive Freight Index revealed a sharp shift in procurement strategies, with annual contracts falling from 80% of its automotive bids in September 2025 to just 45% a year later.\nIn food and beverage, the proportion dropped from 81% to 51% by August as shippers sought “greater flexibility amid volatile transport costs”.\nDiesel prices exceeded $6 a gallon in September for the first time, fuel costs rising considerably faster than freight rates, which EASE reported had increased 27% year on year, compared with a 54% surge in diesel prices.\nRefrigerated linehaul rates, meanwhile, were up 24% at the end of August, against a 50% increase in fuel costs.\nFuel’s share of carrier operating costs soared, from 20% in July to 28% in September, leaving operators exposed to rising expenditure before higher rates could be negotiated.\n“Most companies shipping freight have recognised there is a longer-term cost impact that can’t be ignored,” said EASE chief strategy officer Ken Adamo.\nAlthough contract pricing is beginning to catch up, with EASE’s contract cost per mile rising 6.5% between Q2 and Q3, spot rates declined 2% over the same period.\nRegional disparities are also emerging, particularly in the south-east, where electric vehicle-related freight lanes into Georgia, Tennessee, and Alabama recorded average year-on-year rate growth of 35.6%, compared with 27.4% nationally.\n“Due to the current industry conditions, we are advising companies to price and review Georgia, Tennessee, and Alabama lanes separately from their national network, and check them monthly rather than once a year,” said EASE director of revenue management and market intelligence Alex Perry.\nRefrigerated freight is facing additional capacity pressures, with EASE citing DAT research suggesting carrier numbers are declining more rapidly in the sector, partly because of English language proficiency enforcement and other service initiatives in the southern states.\nThe resulting constraints could extend beyond seasonal produce movements into markets requiring refrigerated trailers for freeze protection, even as overall freight capacity eases.\nAnd the findings echo concerns in Europe, where the IRU recently warned that record road freight rates were masking deteriorating haulier margins, again as operating costs outpace contract pricing. And shippers there are now changing contract strategies accordingly.\nRather than relying on traditional annual tenders, EASE advised shorter contract terms, regional and mid-cycle bids, and closer scrutiny of fuel surcharge mechanisms to manage volatility.\n“If capacity floods back and demand stays flat, spot stays stuck and contract eventually tops out,” said Mr Adamo.\n“If capacity stays disciplined and we get even a modest demand catalyst, things could get interesting quickly.”",
